What is Time Complexity?

Time Complexity is the measure of how the running time of an algorithm grows as the input size (n) increases.

It does not measure actual seconds; it measures the number of operations an algorithm performs.

Goal: Write algorithms that scale efficiently for large inputs.

Big O Notation

Big O represents the worst-case time complexity of an algorithm.

Complexity
Name - Efficiency - O(1)
Constant - ⭐ Excellent - O(log n)
Logarithmic - ⭐ Excellent - O(n)
Linear - ✅ Good - O(n log n)
Linearithmic - ✅ Good - O(n²)
Quadratic - ⚠ Slow-O(2ⁿ)
Exponential-❌ Very Slow-
O(n!)-Factorial - ❌ Extremely Slow

1. O(1) — Constant Time

The execution time never changes, regardless of input size.

Example: Accessing an array element.

int value = arr[5];

10 elements → 1 operation

1,000,000 elements → Still 1 operation

Best possible complexity.

2. O(log n) — Logarithmic Time

The problem size is divided in half repeatedly.

Example: Binary Search

while(low <= high){
    int mid = (low + high)/2;
}

Elements and Maximum Steps
8=3
16=4
1,024=10
1,000,000 ~20

Very efficient for sorted data.

3. O(n) — Linear Time

Every element is visited once.

Example: Linear Search

for(int i=0;i<n;i++)
    if(arr[i]==target) return i;

If n = 1000, about 1000 operations are needed.

4. O(n log n) — Linearithmic

A combination of linear traversal and logarithmic division.

Examples

Merge Sort

Heap Sort

Efficient sorting algorithms

sort(arr.begin(), arr.end());

The STL sort() uses O(n log n) on average.

5. O(n²) — Quadratic Time

Nested loops usually produce quadratic complexity.

for(int i=0;i<n;i++)
    for(int j=0;j<n;j++)
        cout<<i+j;

n and Operations
10 = 100
100 = 10,000
1,000 = 1,000,000

Common in Bubble, Selection, and Insertion Sort (worst case).

6. O(2ⁿ) — Exponential Time

Every choice branches into two more choices.

Example: Recursive Fibonacci

int fib(int n){
    if(n<=1) return n;
    return fib(n-1)+fib(n-2);
}

Growth becomes enormous even for small n.

7. O(n!) — Factorial Time

Used when generating all permutations.

next_permutation(arr.begin(), arr.end());

This is among the slowest possible complexities.

Graph of Growth

As n increases, algorithms with higher complexity grow much faster.

Time Complexity of Common Algorithms

Algorithm & Complexity
Array Access--O(1)
Push Back (Vector)--O(1) Average
Linear Search--O(n)
Binary Search--O(log n)
Bubble Sort--O(n²)
Selection Sort--O(n²)

Insertion Sort--O(n²)

Merge Sort--O(n log n)

Heap Sort--O(n log n)
DFS / BFS -- O(V + E)

Best, Average & Worst Case

Case--Meaning

Best--Minimum time taken

Average--Expected running time

Worst--Maximum time taken (Big O)

Example: Linear Search

Best → First element found → O(1)

Average → Middle element → O(n)

Worst → Last element/not found → O(n)
